    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the pattern intent.\n\nStep 1 — Intercept calls before reaching the real service.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface ImageLoader {\n  load(url: string): Promise<string>;\n}\n\nclass RealImageLoader implements ImageLoader {\n  async load(url: string): Promise<string> {\n    return `loaded:${url}`;\n  }\n}\n\nclass CachedImageProxy implements ImageLoader {\n  private readonly cache = new Map<string, string>();\n\n  constructor(private readonly target: ImageLoader) {}\n\n  async load(url: string): Promise<string> {\n    const cached = this.cache.get(url);\n    if (cached !== undefined) return cached;\n\n    const value = await this.target.load(url);\n    this.cache.set(url, value);\n    return value;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / JavaScript Proxy:\n```ts\nfunction createValidatedProxy(\n  target: { save(value: string): void },\n): typeof target {\n  return new Proxy(target, {\n    get(object, property, receiver) {\n      return Reflect.get(object, property, receiver);\n    },\n  });\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nRepeated image loads can return cached results without changing the real loader.\n\nTrade-off:\nProxy centralizes cross-cutting behavior but can make control flow less obvious when many layers intercept the same call.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the pattern reduces a specific kind of coupling and when a simpler alternative is better.",
