    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the pattern intent.\n\nStep 1 — Define a common component contract.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface FileNode {\n  size(): number;\n}\n\nclass FileItem implements FileNode {\n  constructor(private readonly bytes: number) {}\n\n  size(): number {\n    return this.bytes;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Folder implements FileNode {\n  private readonly children: FileNode[] = [];\n\n  add(child: FileNode): void {\n    this.children.push(child);\n  }\n\n  size(): number {\n    return this.children.reduce(\n      (total, child) => total + child.size(),\n      0,\n    );\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / array composition:\n```ts\ninterface FileNode {\n  size(): number;\n}\n\nfunction totalSize(nodes: readonly FileNode[]): number {\n  return nodes.reduce(\n    (total, node) => total + node.size(),\n    0,\n  );\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nA folder and a file both implement `size()`, so the caller does not need separate traversal logic for each case.\n\nTrade-off:\nComposite simplifies tree operations but can blur the difference between leaves and containers when their capabilities are very different.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the pattern reduces a specific kind of coupling and when a simpler alternative is better.",
