    title: "Flyweight",
    difficulty: "Easy",
    experienceLevel: "0–2 Years",
    category: "Design Patterns",
    expectedAnswer: "Flyweight reduces memory usage by sharing immutable intrinsic state while keeping context-specific extrinsic state outside the shared object.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the pattern intent.\n\nStep 1 — Cache shared formatting objects.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass TextStyle {\n  constructor(\n    readonly font: string,\n    readonly color: string,\n  ) {}\n}\n\nclass TextStyleFactory {\n  private readonly styles = new Map<string, TextStyle>();\n\n  get(font: string, color: string): TextStyle {\n    const key = `${font}|${color}`;\n    const existing = this.styles.get(key);\n\n    if (existing) return existing;\n\n    const style = new TextStyle(font, color);\n    this.styles.set(key, style);\n    return style;\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / Map cache:\n```ts\nclass TextStyle {\n  constructor(\n    readonly font: string,\n    readonly color: string,\n  ) {}\n}\n\nconst styleCache = new Map<string, TextStyle>();\n\nfunction getStyle(\n  font: string,\n  color: string,\n): TextStyle {\n  const key = `${font}|${color}`;\n  const cached = styleCache.get(key);\n  if (cached) return cached;\n\n  const style = new TextStyle(font, color);\n  styleCache.set(key, style);\n  return style;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n1000 labels using the same font/color can share one `TextStyle` instance.\n\nTrade-off:\nFlyweight saves memory when many objects share identical intrinsic state, but cache management adds complexity.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the pattern reduces a specific kind of coupling and when a simpler alternative is better.",
