    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the pattern intent.\n\nStep 1 — Define a common strategy function.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ntype PriceStrategy = (amount: number) => number;\n\nconst regularPrice: PriceStrategy = (amount) => amount;\nconst memberPrice: PriceStrategy = (amount) => amount * 0.9;\n\nfunction checkout(\n  amount: number,\n  strategy: PriceStrategy,\n): number {\n  return strategy(amount);\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / strategy map:\n```ts\ntype PriceStrategy = (amount: number) => number;\n\nconst regularPrice: PriceStrategy = (amount) => amount;\nconst memberPrice: PriceStrategy = (amount) => amount * 0.9;\n\nconst pricingStrategies: Record<\n  \"regular\" | \"member\",\n  PriceStrategy\n> = {\n  regular: regularPrice,\n  member: memberPrice,\n};\n```\n\nExample:\nSwitching from regular pricing to member pricing does not modify `checkout`.\n\nTrade-off:\nStrategy avoids large conditional branches, but creating dozens of tiny strategies can make simple logic harder to follow.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the pattern reduces a specific kind of coupling and when a simpler alternative is better.",
