    title: "Observer",
    difficulty: "Easy",
    experienceLevel: "0–2 Years",
    category: "Design Patterns",
    expectedAnswer: "Observer lets one publisher notify multiple subscribers when its state or event stream changes. In frontend systems it appears in event buses, stores, and subscription APIs.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the pattern intent.\n\nStep 1 — Keep a subscriber set and notify all listeners.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ntype Listener<T> = (value: T) => void;\n\nclass ObservableValue<T> {\n  private readonly listeners = new Set<Listener<T>>();\n  private value: T;\n\n  constructor(initial: T) {\n    this.value = initial;\n  }\n\n  subscribe(listener: Listener<T>): () => void {\n    this.listeners.add(listener);\n    return () => this.listeners.delete(listener);\n  }\n\n  set(value: T): void {\n    this.value = value;\n\n    for (const listener of this.listeners) {\n      listener(value);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / usage:\n```ts\ntype Listener<T> = (value: T) => void;\n\nclass ObservableValue<T> {\n  private readonly listeners = new Set<Listener<T>>();\n  private value: T;\n\n  constructor(initial: T) {\n    this.value = initial;\n  }\n\n  subscribe(listener: Listener<T>): () => void {\n    this.listeners.add(listener);\n    return () => this.listeners.delete(listener);\n  }\n\n  set(value: T): void {\n    this.value = value;\n    for (const listener of this.listeners) listener(value);\n  }\n}\n\nconst count = new ObservableValue(0);\n```\n\nExample:\nReact stores and custom event streams can expose a subscribe/unsubscribe contract.\n\nTrade-off:\nObservers reduce direct coupling but require careful cleanup to avoid leaks and stale listeners.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the pattern reduces a specific kind of coupling and when a simpler alternative is better.",
