    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the pattern intent.\n\nStep 1 — Give each command an executable contract.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface Command {\n  execute(): void;\n  undo(): void;\n}\n\nclass ToggleFlagCommand implements Command {\n  constructor(\n    private readonly state: { enabled: boolean },\n  ) {}\n\n  execute(): void {\n    this.state.enabled = true;\n  }\n\n  undo(): void {\n    this.state.enabled = false;\n  }\n}\n\nclass CommandHistory {\n  private readonly history: Command[] = [];\n\n  run(command: Command): void {\n    command.execute();\n    this.history.push(command);\n  }\n\n  undo(): void {\n    this.history.pop()?.undo();\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / function command:\n```ts\ninterface Command {\n  execute(): void;\n  undo(): void;\n}\n\nfunction createCommand(\n  execute: () => void,\n  undo: () => void,\n): Command {\n  return { execute, undo };\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nRun a command, then `undo()` to reverse it without the caller knowing the implementation details.\n\nTrade-off:\nCommand supports undo and queuing very well, but it adds objects/functions for actions that may be trivial otherwise.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the pattern reduces a specific kind of coupling and when a simpler alternative is better.",
