    number: "ARCH-M2-16",
    title: "State",
    difficulty: "Easy",
    experienceLevel: "0–2 Years",
    category: "Design Patterns",
    expectedAnswer: "State lets an object change its behavior when its internal state changes. In frontend code, an explicit state machine is often clearer than many boolean flags.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the pattern intent.\n\nStep 1 — Model state transitions explicitly.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ntype RequestState = \"idle\" | \"loading\" | \"success\" | \"error\";\n\nclass RequestMachine {\n  private state: RequestState = \"idle\";\n\n  start(): void {\n    if (this.state !== \"idle\") return;\n    this.state = \"loading\";\n  }\n\n  succeed(): void {\n    if (this.state !== \"loading\") return;\n    this.state = \"success\";\n  }\n\n  fail(): void {\n    if (this.state !== \"loading\") return;\n    this.state = \"error\";\n  }\n\n  getState(): RequestState {\n    return this.state;\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / union-state helper:\n```ts\ntype RequestState = \"idle\" | \"loading\" | \"success\" | \"error\";\n\nfunction nextState(\n  current: RequestState,\n  event: \"start\" | \"success\" | \"error\",\n): RequestState {\n  if (current === \"idle\" && event === \"start\") return \"loading\";\n  if (current === \"loading\" && event === \"success\") return \"success\";\n  if (current === \"loading\" && event === \"error\") return \"error\";\n  return current;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nidle → loading → success.\nInvalid transitions are ignored.\n\nTrade-off:\nExplicit state machines reduce impossible states but can add ceremony for a simple boolean state.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the pattern reduces a specific kind of coupling and when a simpler alternative is better.",
