    number: "ARCH-M2-17",
    title: "Mediator",
    difficulty: "Easy",
    experienceLevel: "0–2 Years",
    category: "Design Patterns",
    expectedAnswer: "Mediator centralizes communication between collaborating objects so they do not need direct references to each other. It is useful when many components otherwise form a dense web of dependencies.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the pattern intent.\n\nStep 1 — Route messages through one coordinator.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface Mediator {\n  notify(sender: string, message: string): void;\n}\n\nclass ChatMediator implements Mediator {\n  private readonly users = new Map<string, User>();\n\n  register(user: User): void {\n    this.users.set(user.name, user);\n  }\n\n  notify(sender: string, message: string): void {\n    for (const [name, user] of this.users) {\n      if (name !== sender) {\n        user.receive(`${sender}: ${message}`);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nclass User {\n  constructor(\n    readonly name: string,\n    private readonly mediator: Mediator,\n  ) {}\n\n  send(message: string): void {\n    this.mediator.notify(this.name, message);\n  }\n\n  receive(message: string): void {\n    console.log(message);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / registry approach:\n```ts\nconst channels = new Map<string, Set<string>>();\n\nfunction subscribe(channel: string, id: string): void {\n  const subscribers = channels.get(channel) ?? new Set<string>();\n  subscribers.add(id);\n  channels.set(channel, subscribers);\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nChat users do not know each other directly; they communicate through the mediator.\n\nTrade-off:\nMediator reduces pairwise coupling but can become a central \"god object\" if too much behavior is moved into it.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the pattern reduces a specific kind of coupling and when a simpler alternative is better.",
