    number: "ARCH-M2-18",
    title: "Visitor",
    difficulty: "Easy",
    experienceLevel: "0–2 Years",
    category: "Design Patterns",
    expectedAnswer: "Visitor separates an operation from the object structure it operates on. It is useful when the structure is stable but many different operations need to be added over time.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the pattern intent.\n\nStep 1 — Define a stable node interface with visitor dispatch.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface Visitor {\n  visitText(node: TextNode): number;\n  visitImage(node: ImageNode): number;\n}\n\ninterface DocumentNode {\n  accept(visitor: Visitor): number;\n}\n\nclass TextNode implements DocumentNode {\n  constructor(readonly value: string) {}\n\n  accept(visitor: Visitor): number {\n    return visitor.visitText(this);\n  }\n}\n\nclass ImageNode implements DocumentNode {\n  constructor(readonly url: string) {}\n\n  accept(visitor: Visitor): number {\n    return visitor.visitImage(this);\n  }\n}\n\nclass CountVisitor implements Visitor {\n  visitText(_: TextNode): number {\n    return 1;\n  }\n\n  visitImage(_: ImageNode): number {\n    return 1;\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / plain traversal alternative:\n```ts\ninterface DocumentNode {\n  kind: \"text\" | \"image\";\n}\n\nfunction countNodes(\n  nodes: readonly DocumentNode[],\n): number {\n  return nodes.length;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nAdd a new operation such as accessibility analysis without changing each node's core data.\n\nTrade-off:\nVisitor makes new operations easy but makes adding new node types harder because the visitor interface must change.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the pattern reduces a specific kind of coupling and when a simpler alternative is better.",
