    experienceLevel: "0–2 Years",
    category: "Design Patterns",
    expectedAnswer: "Iterator provides a standard way to traverse a collection without exposing its internal representation. JavaScript already implements this protocol through iterable objects and generators.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the pattern intent.\n\nStep 1 — Implement an iterable collection.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass NumberRange implements Iterable<number> {\n  constructor(\n    private readonly start: number,\n    private readonly end: number,\n  ) {}\n\n  *[Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<number> {\n    for (let value = this.start; value <= this.end; value++) {\n      yield value;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nclass NumberRange implements Iterable<number> {\n  constructor(\n    private readonly start: number,\n    private readonly end: number,\n  ) {}\n\n  *[Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<number> {\n    for (let value = this.start; value <= this.end; value++) {\n      yield value;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nconst range = new NumberRange(1, 3);\n\nfor (const value of range) {\n  console.log(value);\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nThe caller uses `for...of` without knowing how the values are produced.\n\nTrade-off:\nIterators support lazy traversal and memory efficiency, but generator/iterator control flow can be less familiar to beginners.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the pattern reduces a specific kind of coupling and when a simpler alternative is better.",
